<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charioteer of Delphi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 475 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charioteer of Delphi is a life-size bronze statue of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> driver made around 475 BCE, part of a group dedicated at Delphi by Polyzalus, tyrant of Gela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, for a victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a>. Buried by a rock fall, it was found in 1896 and is one of the finest surviving Greek bronzes of the early Classical period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charioteer-of-delphi.jpg" alt="Charioteer of Delphi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunnar Bach Pedersen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vognstyreren-fra_Delfi2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
